Allocation vs Market Contribution
The majority of excess return over the period was driven by allocation decisions rather than passive market exposure. With a beta of approximately 0.40, the portfolio maintained limited directional exposure during the weaker early phase, reducing downside participation. As conditions improved, increased effective exposure allowed for stronger capture of upside, resulting in positive excess return despite an uneven market environment.


Regime / Timing Attribution
Performance over the period can be decomposed into two distinct phases. During the initial phase, characterised by weaker and less stable price action, exposure remained deliberately constrained. This limited drawdown and preserved capital during a period where directional conviction was low.

In the latter phase, as trend conditions improved and price action became more constructive, the portfolio increased its effective participation, capturing a greater share of upside. This transition from defensive positioning to selective participation was the primary driver of performance recovery and end-of-period outperformance.


Return Drivers
Returns were driven by a combination of disciplined exposure management and selective trend participation. Reduced exposure during unstable market conditions limited unnecessary volatility, while increased participation during periods of improving momentum enabled efficient capture of directional moves.

Volatility control also played a key role, with lower realised volatility during the early phase contributing to a more stable equity curve and improving overall risk-adjusted performance. As conditions stabilised, the portfolio was able to convert directional opportunities into returns more efficiently.


What DIDN’T work
While overall performance was positive, early-period conditions limited the ability to deploy capital effectively, resulting in reduced participation during initial market movements. Additionally, rising cross-asset correlations during parts of the period reduced diversification benefits, meaning that return opportunities were more concentrated rather than broad-based.


